 graph from $t=0$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=1+4=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121014/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49319"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/assets/aad42b26d165789da802c0f507e3445976f3269b4c77e8b47201fb043b08f1f6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=6.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=6$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=3.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(3)=6+5=11\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-121011/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "1e50e90d-9111-5657-8aff-bd40208e73b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121011/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "3a5721c4-0839-5a8a-a15e-557f8f093ccd" :an